Track 2: Oleg at Central Interchange
- Story & Context: Aging gatekeeper Oleg Rostov spent thirty-two shipboard cycles validating passage chits at Central Interchange, listening to the chime of departure bells that only led to identical rings of recycled air and rusted rivets. Smuggling a pocket metallophone fashioned from scrap heat sinks into his glass inspection booth, Rostov wept into his grease-caked intercom while watching his daughter board the final outbound colonist shuttle to the engine rings. "I punch your ticket for a room without dirt, so hold your tin bell close to your shirt," he crooned into the microphone, allowing the cold titanium ring of his improvised glockenspiel to bleed into the tape alongside the deep hydraulic groan of the departure gates. Cut during an unmonitored graveyard shift, the master reel preserved the faint, terrifying hiss of an unsealed air gasket that Oleg refused to patch until his final verse was recorded.
